96
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:52,662
Fluorinated polyimide,

97
00:03:52,662 --> 00:03:54,360
used in phone screens.

98
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:55,800
Photoresist.

99
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:58,422
And ultra-pure hydrogen fluoride,

100
00:03:58,422 --> 00:04:01,140
used to clean and etch wafers.

101
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:03,586
Korea is home to two of the

102
00:04:03,586 --> 00:04:05,918
world's biggest memory makers,

103
00:04:05,918 --> 00:04:08,090
Samsung and SK hynix.

104
00:04:08,250 --> 00:04:09,578
And Japan supplied,

105
00:04:09,578 --> 00:04:10,906
by one estimate,

106
00:04:10,906 --> 00:04:13,382
about seventy percent or more of these

107
00:04:13,382 --> 00:04:15,782
materials to the whole world.

108
00:04:16,017 --> 00:04:18,347
Korea scrambled.

109
00:04:18,407 --> 00:04:20,507
Samsung stepped up testing of resists and

110
00:04:20,507 --> 00:04:22,687
hydrogen fluoride from other suppliers.

111
00:04:22,687 --> 00:04:25,198
A Korean chemical maker,

112
00:04:25,198 --> 00:04:26,017
Soulbrain,

113
00:04:26,017 --> 00:04:28,838
raced to match the purity of the

114
00:04:28,838 --> 00:04:30,567
Japanese gas.

115
00:04:30,697 --> 00:04:32,590
Shipments from Japan now needed

116
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:34,847
case-by-case permission.

117
00:04:35,067 --> 00:04:36,116
For the first time,

118
00:04:36,116 --> 00:04:38,257
the chip industry saw a supply of

119
00:04:38,257 --> 00:04:40,998
chemicals turned into a lever.

120
00:04:41,250 --> 00:04:43,510
Did Korea break free?

121
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:44,540
Partly.

122
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,026
According to Korean industry reports,

123
00:04:47,026 --> 00:04:49,139
it now makes more of the ordinary

124
00:04:49,139 --> 00:04:50,700
materials at home.

125
00:04:50,990 --> 00:04:53,039
But for the most advanced chips,

126
00:04:53,039 --> 00:04:54,790
the pattern repeats.

127
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:56,265
The finer the circuits,

128
00:04:56,265 --> 00:04:58,302
the more Korea depends on Japanese

129
00:04:58,302 --> 00:04:59,920
materials again.

130
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:02,968
Every new generation of chips moves the

131
00:05:02,968 --> 00:05:04,220
finish line.

132
00:05:04,484 --> 00:05:07,934
A chemical can be restricted.

133
00:05:07,994 --> 00:05:11,728
A gas can simply disappear.

134
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:20,193
Why would a war in Ukraine threaten

135
00:05:20,193 --> 00:05:22,977
chip factories in Taiwan?

136
00:05:26,384 --> 00:05:28,214
Because of neon.

137
00:05:28,274 --> 00:05:30,895
Chipmakers use ultra-pure neon gas inside

138
00:05:30,895 --> 00:05:34,354
the lasers of their lithography machines.

139
00:05:34,484 --> 00:05:36,822
And in early twenty twenty-two,

140
00:05:36,822 --> 00:05:38,687
two Ukrainian companies,

141
00:05:38,687 --> 00:05:41,733
Ingas in Mariupol and Cryoin in Odessa,

142
00:05:41,733 --> 00:05:42,654
produced,

143
00:05:42,654 --> 00:05:44,520
by Reuters' calculation,

144
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:47,117
forty-five to fifty-four percent of the

145
00:05:47,117 --> 00:05:49,134
world's neon for chipmaking.

146
00:05:49,254 --> 00:05:51,048
When Russia invaded,

147
00:05:51,048 --> 00:05:52,428
both shut down.

148
00:05:52,684 --> 00:05:54,454
Why Ukraine?

149
00:05:54,524 --> 00:05:57,224
Because neon is a by-product.

150
00:05:57,514 --> 00:05:59,882
It is collected from steelmaking,

151
00:05:59,882 --> 00:06:03,231
and historically up to ninety percent of

152
00:06:03,231 --> 00:06:05,384
the neon for the chip industry came

153
00:06:05,384 --> 00:06:07,991
as a by-product of Russian steel plants,

154
00:06:07,991 --> 00:06:10,861
with companies in Ukraine turning it into

155
00:06:10,861 --> 00:06:13,614
the ultra-pure gas chipmakers need.

156
00:06:13,924 --> 00:06:16,565
The whole chip industry used about about

157
00:06:16,565 --> 00:06:18,393
five hundred and forty tons of it

158
00:06:18,393 --> 00:06:20,008
in twenty twenty-one.

159
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:22,910
The big chipmakers survived.

160
00:06:23,050 --> 00:06:25,119
They had learned from an earlier scare

161
00:06:25,119 --> 00:06:26,475
in twenty fourteen,

162
00:06:26,475 --> 00:06:28,521
when Russia annexed Crimea,

163
00:06:28,521 --> 00:06:30,500
and kept safety stocks.

164
00:06:30,710 --> 00:06:33,460
But replacing the lost supply was slow.

165
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:36,210
Building new neon purification lines

166
00:06:36,210 --> 00:06:38,379
elsewhere could take nine months to two

167
00:06:38,379 --> 00:06:39,220
years.

168
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,552
Neon showed that a chokepoint does not

169
00:06:41,552 --> 00:06:44,070
need a government to switch it off.

170
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:46,202
A war is enough.

171
00:06:46,450 --> 00:06:47,158
In fact,

172
00:06:47,158 --> 00:06:49,574
after the scare of twenty fourteen,

173
00:06:49,574 --> 00:06:52,502
the industry had already started to spread

174
00:06:52,502 --> 00:06:53,590
its bets.

175
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:56,506
By one consultant's estimate at Bain,

176
00:06:56,506 --> 00:06:59,258
only about two-fifths of the neon used

177
00:06:59,258 --> 00:07:01,259
for chips still came from Russia and

178
00:07:01,259 --> 00:07:04,360
Ukraine by the time of the invasion.

179
00:07:04,510 --> 00:07:06,700
The lesson had been learned once.

180
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:08,776
It had to be learned again.

181
00:07:09,017 --> 00:07:10,806
And then came the metal,

182
00:07:10,806 --> 00:07:13,658
controlled by the country that wanted it

183
00:07:13,658 --> 00:07:15,035
controlled.

184
00:07:20,684 --> 00:07:23,398
What happens when one country produces

185
00:07:23,398 --> 00:07:25,897
almost all of something?

186
00:07:29,684 --> 00:07:30,654
Meet gallium,

187
00:07:30,654 --> 00:07:33,389
a soft silver metal used in special

188
00:07:33,389 --> 00:07:35,105
chips for defense systems,

189
00:07:35,105 --> 00:07:37,364
telecom equipment and LEDs.

190
00:07:37,364 --> 00:07:39,606
In the United States,

191
00:07:39,606 --> 00:07:41,877
chips use about four in five of

192
00:07:41,877 --> 00:07:44,464
all the gallium consumed.

193
00:07:44,614 --> 00:07:47,491
And according to the US Geological Survey,

194
00:07:47,491 --> 00:07:50,181
China produced ninety-nine percent of the

195
00:07:50,181 --> 00:07:52,850
world's raw gallium in twenty twenty-four.

196
00:07:53,084 --> 00:07:55,033
In August twenty twenty-three,

197
00:07:55,033 --> 00:07:58,098
China began requiring licences for every

198
00:07:58,098 --> 00:07:59,524
gallium export.

199
00:07:59,524 --> 00:08:01,064
Prices jumped.

200
00:08:01,064 --> 00:08:03,168
Raw gallium in China went from about

201
00:08:03,168 --> 00:08:05,693
two hundred and forty dollars a kilogram

202
00:08:05,693 --> 00:08:08,008
in mid twenty twenty-three to four hundred

203
00:08:08,008 --> 00:08:10,323
and twenty dollars by October twenty

204
00:08:10,323 --> 00:08:11,554
twenty-four.

205
00:08:11,634 --> 00:08:12,026
Then,

206
00:08:12,026 --> 00:08:14,067
in December twenty twenty-four,

207
00:08:14,067 --> 00:08:16,747
China banned gallium exports to the United

208
00:08:16,747 --> 00:08:18,584
States altogether.

209
00:08:18,817 --> 00:08:19,967
A year later,

210
00:08:19,967 --> 00:08:22,549
after talks between the American and

211
00:08:22,549 --> 00:08:23,934
Chinese presidents,

212
00:08:23,934 --> 00:08:26,177
China suspended that ban.

213
00:08:26,277 --> 00:08:28,157
But it only paused it.

214
00:08:28,387 --> 00:08:30,623
Exporters still need a licence from

215
00:08:30,623 --> 00:08:32,835
