229
00:08:59,017 --> 00:09:00,605
The third is policy:

230
00:09:00,605 --> 00:09:03,253
China produces almost all the raw gallium

231
00:09:03,253 --> 00:09:05,397
and decides who gets it.

232
00:09:05,597 --> 00:09:07,657
Each one is fixed in a different

233
00:09:07,657 --> 00:09:08,048
way,

234
00:09:08,048 --> 00:09:09,884
and none of them quickly.

235
00:09:10,150 --> 00:09:13,789
So what are governments doing about it?

236
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:22,236
Can a country protect its chemicals like

237
00:09:22,236 --> 00:09:24,356
a strategic asset?

238
00:09:28,450 --> 00:09:30,900
Japan answered that question with a

239
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:31,990
purchase.

240
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:33,731
In twenty twenty-four,

241
00:09:33,731 --> 00:09:35,621
a government-backed fund,

242
00:09:35,621 --> 00:09:38,229
the Japan Investment Corporation,

243
00:09:38,229 --> 00:09:38,923
bought JSR,

244
00:09:38,923 --> 00:09:41,291
one of the three big resist makers,

245
00:09:41,291 --> 00:09:43,444
in a deal worth about about six

246
00:09:43,444 --> 00:09:45,480
point four billion dollars.

247
00:09:45,700 --> 00:09:48,140
JSR left the stock market.

248
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,456
A private chemical company became,

249
00:09:50,456 --> 00:09:51,183
in effect,

250
00:09:51,183 --> 00:09:52,900
a national asset.

251
00:09:52,970 --> 00:09:55,713
JSR had already bought an American

252
00:09:55,713 --> 00:09:56,436
start-up,

253
00:09:56,436 --> 00:09:57,160
Inpria,

254
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:59,404
for five hundred and fourteen million

255
00:09:59,404 --> 00:10:00,127
dollars,

256
00:10:00,127 --> 00:10:02,621
